# Configuration arrives as environment variables, never baked into the image.
# docker-compose.yml sets these for you; copy to .env only for running outside Docker.

DATABASE_URL=postgres://recon:recon@localhost:5432/recon

# Who may call the management endpoints, and what each of them may do.
#
# A comma-separated list of "principal:secret:grant|grant". Every key belongs to a named
# principal, because the evidence endpoints record who read what and one shared key makes
# that record a list of the word "api" (ADR-0066). Two grants exist:
#
#   evidence.raw      may read the bytes of a stored document
#   evidence.export   may take a copy out of the system
#
# The service refuses to start without this, and refuses to start if the old RECON_API_KEY
# is still set.
RECON_API_KEYS=amaka@example.com:local-dev-key-0123456789,auditor@example.com:local-dev-audit-0123456789:evidence.raw|evidence.export

# The root key evidence is encrypted under, as "<key-id>:<base64 of 32 bytes>".
#
#   echo "k1:$(openssl rand -base64 32)"
#
# Evidence is encrypted per record before it is stored and there is deliberately no
# unencrypted path (ADR-0063), so the service refuses to start without one. A deployment on
# a KMS replaces the key ring in apps/*/vault.ts and leaves everything else alone.
RECON_EVIDENCE_KEY=k1:AAECAwQFBgcICQoLDA0ODxAREhMUFRYXGBkaGxwdHh8=

# Keys that no longer seal new records but must still unwrap old ones. A retired key stays
# here for as long as anything sealed under it is still within its retention: dropping it
# early does not delete that evidence, it makes it unreadable.
# RECON_EVIDENCE_KEYS_RETIRED=k0:<base64>

# How long each version of a document is kept, in days. The defaults are a starting position
# and not a legal opinion — the redacted horizon in particular is a question for counsel
# against CBN and FIRS record-keeping obligations (ADR-0065).
# RECON_RETENTION_ORIGINAL_DAYS=30      # the dispute window, after which payloads are reduced
# RECON_RETENTION_REDACTED_DAYS=2192    # six years: the financial record horizon
# RECON_RETENTION_INBOX_DAYS=30         # stragglers the drain never worked

# How long an export link lives. Minutes, not days: a link valid for a week gets forwarded.
# RECON_EXPORT_TTL_MS=900000

# What each provider signs its deliveries with. Webhooks authenticate by signature and by
# nothing else. One variable per source; a source with no secret here answers 503 and
# stores nothing.
RECON_WEBHOOK_SECRET_PAYSTACK=sk_test_demo_secret
RECON_WEBHOOK_SECRET_FLUTTERWAVE=flw_test_demo_secret_hash

# The outgoing secret, during a rotation.
#
# A delivery is verified twice — at the door, and again by the worker that interprets it —
# so rotating a secret while a backlog exists used to make every pending delivery signed
# with the old one fail its second check, and the worker's answer to that is `rejected`,
# which is terminal. Real payments, discarded, because a credential was rotated on a busy
# afternoon (ADR-0073).
#
# The rotation is therefore four steps, with a wait in the middle:
#   1. set _PREVIOUS to the current secret
#   2. set the current secret to the new one
#   3. wait for /health to report inbox.pending == 0
#   4. remove _PREVIOUS
# RECON_WEBHOOK_SECRET_PAYSTACK_PREVIOUS=sk_test_old_secret

# Whose books these are, and which of our own accounts a statement upload is about when the
# request does not say. Fee contracts are negotiated per merchant.
RECON_MERCHANT=demo-merchant
RECON_BANK_ACCOUNT=gtb-3011
RECON_BANK=gtbank

# The inbox worker. The interval is how long a delivery may sit accepted but not yet booked;
# the batch is how many one pass works. Both are visible in /health as the pending depth.
#
# A delivery that throws does NOT retry on this interval — it backs off exponentially with
# deterministic jitter, so eight attempts span minutes rather than four seconds and a
# deadlock cannot retire an authentic payment (ADR-0073).
# RECON_DRAIN_INTERVAL_MS=500
# RECON_DRAIN_BATCH=100
# RECON_DRAIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS=8

# ── Reconciling on a schedule ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# **Zero, the default, means nothing in this process reconciles.** The webhook rail drains
# itself and the uploads happen when a file arrives, but exceptions are only ever raised by
# a run — so a deployment that sets neither this nor an external cron never surfaces an
# anomaly, and the empty queue reads exactly like a clean set of books (ADR-0074).
#
# Set this, or drive POST /reconcile/runs from a cron. Not both, and not on more than one
# replica: a concurrent second run duplicates nothing (every write is keyed) but does
# duplicate the work. The service logs a warning at boot when this is unset, and /health
# reports `reconciliation_stale`.
# RECON_RECONCILE_INTERVAL_MS=900000

# How many records one run may consider. A run that hits this reports it, and stops clearing
# exceptions whose subjects it could not see — the difference between "the problem went away"
# and "we did not look" (ADR-0075). Watch queue.withheld: if it stays non-zero, raise this.
# RECON_RECONCILE_LIMIT=1000

# The bounded subset search, for payouts a PSP reports without per-line references.
#
# 24 candidates is a SMALL-BATCH bound. A payout batching more is escalated as
# BATCH_TOO_LARGE — honestly saying the arithmetic was never attempted — rather than reported
# as unmatched. The search is exponential in the candidate count, so MAX_STEPS is what
# actually stops it: raise them together or you buy `undecidable` rather than answers
# (ADR-0070, docs/PERFORMANCE.md).
# RECON_SUBSET_MAX_CANDIDATES=24
# RECON_SUBSET_MAX_SIZE=12
# RECON_SUBSET_MAX_STEPS=200000

# ── Rate limiting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Per caller, per minute, PER PROCESS. /webhooks/:source is the reason it exists: it is
# unauthenticated by design and the work of deciding a delivery is not authentic — buffering
# the body, HMAC-ing all of it — happens before the 401.
#
# This is the floor, not the control. Two replicas allow twice the rate and a rotating source
# address defeats it entirely; the real limit belongs at a WAF or gateway. Set
# RECON_TRUST_PROXY=true behind a load balancer, or every caller shares its address and this
# becomes a global limit. 0 disables.
# RECON_RATE_WEBHOOK_PER_MINUTE=1200
# RECON_RATE_MANAGEMENT_PER_MINUTE=600
# RECON_RATE_MAX_KEYS=10000
# RECON_TRUST_PROXY=false

# ── When /health stops saying it is fine ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# /health answers 503 once any of these is breached, with a sentence per breach in `alerts`.
# Use it as a readiness or alerting target, never as a liveness probe: a degraded service is
# up, and restarting it fixes nothing. Each 0 disables that verdict.
# RECON_ALERT_INBOX_PENDING=1000           # a worker that stopped, not a busy minute
# RECON_ALERT_INBOX_FAILED=1               # one. Each is a payment nobody will retry.
# RECON_ALERT_INBOX_AGE_MS=900000          # how stale the oldest unworked delivery may get
# RECON_ALERT_OPEN_EXCEPTIONS=500          # past this, a queue stops being one
# RECON_ALERT_RECONCILE_AGE_MS=93600000    # 26h: nothing is surfaced between runs
# RECON_ALERT_ATTESTATION_AGE_MS=604800000 # a week without a human checking the bank

# PORT=8080
# LOG_LEVEL=info
